WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.491
Building with AI runs on a second curriculum

2
00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:06.476
that school rarely teaches:

3
00:00:06.476 --> 00:00:10.261
terminals, files, git, keys, hosting.

4
00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:11.561
If you never learned it,

5
00:00:11.561 --> 00:00:14.917
the gap is in the syllabus, not in you.

6
00:00:14.917 --> 00:00:17.784
This course is the map for it:

7
00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:21.511
what each thing is, and where it goes.

8
00:00:21.511 --> 00:00:23.578
Aaron, who made this course,

9
00:00:23.578 --> 00:00:26.666
has a degree in Information Systems,

10
00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:29.767
and still found out the hard way.

11
00:00:30.218 --> 00:00:32.238
He wrote Python in Notepad

12
00:00:32.238 --> 00:00:35.477
and saved it as a .txt file.

13
00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:38.589
He didn't know to rename it to .py.

14
00:00:38.589 --> 00:00:40.237
He didn't know to open a terminal

15
00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:43.163
and cd into the folder first.

16
00:00:43.163 --> 00:00:45.508
And .venv?

17
00:00:45.508 --> 00:00:47.900
He had no idea what that was.

18
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.549
None of it is hard.

19
00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.847
Nobody had handed it to him.

20
00:00:51.847 --> 00:00:55.179
You probably have your own pile of these, too.

21
00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:57.689
Each one has a place.

22
00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:00.671
Here's why this happens to smart people.

23
00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:04.547
Computer science mostly teaches the ideas:

24
00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:06.683
algorithms, data structures,

25
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:08.448
how computation works.

26
00:01:08.448 --> 00:01:11.385
Information systems mostly teaches

27
00:01:11.385 --> 00:01:14.114
how businesses run on technology:

28
00:01:14.114 --> 00:01:17.469
databases, processes, projects.

29
00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:20.279
The everyday tools fall in the gap between them:

30
00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:24.354
the terminal, the folders, the keys, the hosting.

31
00:01:24.354 --> 00:01:27.558
Used everywhere, rarely taught.

32
00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:30.461
So most people learn them the old way.

33
00:01:30.461 --> 00:01:33.618
Apprenticeship, where someone leans over and says,

34
00:01:33.618 --> 00:01:36.312
you need to cd first.

35
00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:40.828
Forums, where a stranger answers your exact error.

36
00:01:40.828 --> 00:01:42.419
And breaking things.

37
00:01:42.419 --> 00:01:43.928
Lots of things.

38
00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:45.786
It works, slowly.

39
00:01:45.786 --> 00:01:47.283
And everyone ends up

40
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:49.897
with a different set of holes.

41
00:01:50.347 --> 00:01:53.667
Even MIT has a course for this gap:

42
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:58.288
The Missing Semester of Your CS Education.

43
00:01:58.288 --> 00:02:00.436
Its front page says tool skills

44
00:02:00.436 --> 00:02:01.934
are rarely covered in class,

45
00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:04.837
and left to students to figure out on their own.

46
00:02:04.837 --> 00:02:08.180
Its January 2026 edition includes

47
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.433
a lecture on agentic coding.

48
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:13.962
If MIT students need a missing semester,

49
00:02:13.962 --> 00:02:16.296
you're allowed to need a missing map.

50
00:02:16.296 --> 00:02:18.687
It follows you to work, too.

51
00:02:18.687 --> 00:02:20.545
Aaron's automations there ran

52
00:02:20.545 --> 00:02:22.949
under his own personal accounts.

53
00:02:22.949 --> 00:02:24.922
They had to move to service accounts,

54
00:02:24.922 --> 00:02:27.755
with their credentials stored properly.

55
00:02:27.755 --> 00:02:30.404
Nobody had taught him that either.

56
00:02:30.854 --> 00:02:34.337
Here's the shelf: a map with seven districts,

57
00:02:34.337 --> 00:02:36.938
each answering one question.

58
00:02:36.938 --> 00:02:39.898
The Machine: what's on your computer.

59
00:02:39.898 --> 00:02:42.615
The Code: what programs are made of.

60
00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:45.994
The History: how work is saved and shared.

61
00:02:45.994 --> 00:02:48.676
The Network: how computers talk.

62
00:02:48.676 --> 00:02:51.125
Trust: who may do what.

63
00:02:51.125 --> 00:02:53.401
Shipping: getting it to people.

64
00:02:53.401 --> 00:02:55.131
And strung over all of them,

65
00:02:55.131 --> 00:02:56.733
the seventh: the Lanterns.

66
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:58.323
That's AI.

67
00:02:58.323 --> 00:02:59.926
It lights every district,

68
00:02:59.926 --> 00:03:02.526
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

69
00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:04.639
This lesson sits at the gate.

70
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:06.926
And those loose questions?

71
00:03:06.926 --> 00:03:10.758
.py and cd live in The Machine.

72
00:03:10.758 --> 00:03:14.763
.venv lives in The Code.

73
00:03:14.763 --> 00:03:17.969
Service accounts live in Trust.

74
00:03:18.419 --> 00:03:22.366
That's the promise: a map, not a manual.

75
00:03:22.366 --> 00:03:25.373
Every lesson answers five questions:

76
00:03:25.373 --> 00:03:27.672
what is it, where does it live,

77
00:03:27.672 --> 00:03:30.865
what is it like, what does it connect to,

78
00:03:30.865 --> 00:03:32.827
and what will surprise you.

79
00:03:32.827 --> 00:03:35.892
You won't finish able to do the thing yet.

80
00:03:35.892 --> 00:03:38.156
You'll recognise it when you meet it.

81
00:03:38.156 --> 00:03:41.825
Real tools are messier than the map.

82
00:03:41.825 --> 00:03:43.822
Git lives in The History,

83
00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:46.202
but it talks across The Network.

84
00:03:46.202 --> 00:03:49.928
Keys belong to Trust, and they turn up everywhere.

85
00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:53.702
Memorising commands is safe to skip.

86
00:03:53.702 --> 00:03:55.826
Your AI can type them.

87
00:03:55.826 --> 00:03:58.707
Your job is knowing what they mean.

88
00:03:59.157 --> 00:04:01.015
Two surprises you'll meet early.

89
00:04:01.015 --> 00:04:02.594
The first is a word: just.

90
00:04:02.594 --> 00:04:05.043
Just cd into the folder.

91
00:04:05.043 --> 00:04:08.375
Just activate the venv.

92
00:04:08.375 --> 00:04:10.593
Just add it to your PATH.

93
00:04:10.593 --> 00:04:12.323
Tutorials say it.

94
00:04:12.323 --> 00:04:13.867
Coworkers say it.

95
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:16.677
Your AI assistant says it too.

96
00:04:16.677 --> 00:04:19.661
Every just is a lesson somebody skipped.

97
00:04:19.661 --> 00:04:21.344
It isn't a sign you're behind.

98
00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:23.062
It's a pin on the map.

99
00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.942
The second is an error.

100
00:04:25.942 --> 00:04:28.182
Run a script from the wrong place,

101
00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:30.400
and you'll see something like this:

102
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.695
can't open file, no such file or directory.

103
00:04:34.695 --> 00:04:36.762
That doesn't mean your code is broken.

104
00:04:36.762 --> 00:04:38.898
Python never even opened it.

105
00:04:38.898 --> 00:04:41.255
Usually, you're standing in the wrong folder,

106
00:04:41.255 --> 00:04:44.740
or the file is still called .txt.

107
00:04:45.189 --> 00:04:46.930
Three things to keep.

108
00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.926
The gap is in the syllabus, not in you.

109
00:04:49.926 --> 00:04:52.155
Nobody handed you this map;

110
00:04:52.155 --> 00:04:55.766
it's a missing semester, not a character flaw.

111
00:04:55.766 --> 00:04:58.285
Every confusing thing has a shelf:

112
00:04:58.285 --> 00:04:59.388
seven districts,

113
00:04:59.388 --> 00:05:02.709
with AI as the lanterns over all of them.

114
00:05:02.709 --> 00:05:05.681
Aim for recognition, not recipes.

115
00:05:05.681 --> 00:05:09.269
Once you know what a thing is and where it lives,

116
00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:12.196
the how-to is easy to find.
